  "note": "#3022 (research 0028 rows 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6; seat:caa's ruling of 2026-09-26): the completeness gate's G4 is exact. The emitted answers must be exactly the sealed rows that are instances of the goal, where an instance is one binding of the goal's variables that makes the goal STRUCTURALLY equal to the row (a repeated variable binds one value), and two answers are one answer exactly when they are structurally equal. So G4 agrees with the sealed table, a count over it, negation and every engine store, which all compare structurally. Each case gives the goal, the sealed rows of the goal's predicate (the last certificate's table; G2 and G3 are not exercised here, so no certificate is carried), the emitted answers' claims, and the verdict: accept, or the gate's G4 reject tag completeness_set_mismatch together with `mismatch`, the contract's structured result: {extra: literal} or {missing: literal}. #3023 (research 0028 A16, seat:caa's ruling): G4 is the contract's check_answer_set (rs) / CheckAnswerSet (go) / checkAnswerSet (ts), and its precedence is normative: the first extra in claim order, otherwise the first missing row in ground_key order. Hand-authored from design 0008 section 1.4.4 and research 0028's measured rows; no case was captured from a run. Every replayer (contract/rs/tests/exact_answer_set_vectors.rs, contract/go/exact_answer_set_vectors_test.go, contract/ts/test/exact_answer_set_vectors.test.ts) applies every case, counts them against this list, and runs its own core's contract G4. Terms use check.json's notation; `[a]` is the cons spelling .(a, []), and list(a) the flat one.",
